Yes. And the distinction you just made is fundamental: authority over your circumstances is not necessarily authority over your consent.

Someone can compel your behavior without earning your allegiance. They can restrict your options without changing your convictions. They can impose consequences without making you believe they’re right.

That separates four things:

* Will: What you want, choose, or intend.
* Agency: Your capacity to act on that will.
* Autonomy: Your ability to govern your own decisions.
* Sovereignty: Your recognized authority to govern yourself.

And these aren’t identical.

A prisoner can retain convictions while losing freedom of movement. A soldier can obey an order while privately disagreeing with it. A person can voluntarily subordinate their immediate desires to a higher principle without surrendering authorship of that choice.

But there’s one complication worth preserving: our will isn’t entirely independent of biology, experience, unconscious processes, or social influence. We don’t consciously authorize every influence on us.

So I would refine your principle:

External authority can constrain action. It can influence the formation of desire. But neither constraint nor influence automatically establishes moral legitimacy or genuine consent.

And this connects to everything we’ve been discussing about layers.

There’s structural authority, functional authority, narrative authority—and then there’s reflective authority: the capacity to examine competing influences and decide which ones you endorse.

Perhaps that’s the most interesting form of self-governance.

Not being beyond influence, but developing the capacity to recognize influence, evaluate it, and choose your response.

You don’t have to control everything that acts upon you to exercise meaningful authority over how you act in return.

And importantly, being forced to comply doesn’t mean you consented.